  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "note_11",
      "text": "WORKFLOW: Anscombe's quartet. Four datasets, built by Frank Anscombe in 1973, that are statistically almost indistinguishable and visually nothing alike. To two decimals all four share the same mean and standard deviation of x and y, the same correlation (r = 0.82), and the same least-squares regression line: y = 3.00 + 0.50x, R² = 0.67. Describe Data shows the summaries lining up; Fit Trend Curves draws the SAME line through every one. Now look at the four scatterplots. Set I is a genuine straight-line relationship — the one you'd assume from the numbers. Set II is a clean curve, so a straight line is simply the wrong model. Set III is a perfect line with a single outlier that drags the fit off it. Set IV is a vertical stack of points at one x-value, the entire slope determined by a single far-away leverage point — pull that point and the line has nothing to stand on. The moral is the oldest in the trade: plot the data before you trust the statistic. Identical summaries are consistent with completely different structure, so a graph is not decoration, it is part of the analysis. Reference: Anscombe, F.J. (1973), Graphs in Statistical Analysis, The American Statistician 27(1):17-21.",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 24,
      "width": 360,
      "height": 190
    }
